// IELTS Writing Task 2 essay skills: 108 original multiple-choice items on identifying the essay type, writing a
// thesis and introduction, topic sentences, support, linking words, conclusions, formal register and accuracy.
// Each row is [level, sub, question, right answer, three wrong answers, explanation]; the right answer is moved
// to a rotating position so that A to D are used evenly. Everything is original; British spelling throughout.
const R = [
  // ---------- question-type (12) ----------
  [1, 'question-type', `Read this prompt: "Some people think children should start school at the age of four. Others believe that seven is a better age. Discuss both views and give your own opinion." What type of essay is it?`,
    `A discussion essay: both views and your own opinion`, [`A problem-solution essay`, `An advantages and disadvantages essay`, `A two-part question essay`],
    `"Discuss both views and give your own opinion" is the classic wording of a discussion essay. There are no causes or solutions to give, so the problem-solution type is wrong, and nothing asks you to weigh advantages against disadvantages.`],
  [1, 'question-type', `A prompt ends with "To what extent do you agree or disagree?" What must you do?`,
    `State how far you agree and support that position through the essay`, [`Describe both sides equally without giving a view`, `List problems and then suggest solutions`, `Answer two separate questions in two paragraphs`],
    `This wording asks for your position, which can be full or partial agreement. Staying neutral is wrong because the task wants an opinion, and the other two options describe different essay types.`],
  [1, 'question-type', `Prompt: "Many cities have serious traffic problems. What are the causes of this, and what can be done to reduce it?" Which essay type is this?`,
    `Problem-solution (causes and solutions)`, [`Opinion (agree or disagree)`, `Discussion of two views`, `Advantages and disadvantages`],
    `The question asks for causes and then for actions, which is the problem-solution pattern. There are no two views to discuss and no statement to agree with, so the opinion and discussion types do not fit.`],
  [2, 'question-type', `Which prompt is a two-part question essay?`,
    `"Why do more people live alone today? Is this a positive or a negative development?"`, [`"Some people think that exams are unfair. Others disagree. Discuss both views."`, `"Do the advantages of working abroad outweigh the disadvantages?"`, `"To what extent do you agree that museums should be free?"`],
    `It contains two direct questions, and the essay must answer both of them. The other three each use one of the other standard formulas: discussion, advantages-disadvantages and opinion.`],
  [2, 'question-type', `For a prompt that asks "Do the advantages outweigh the disadvantages?", what must your conclusion include?`,
    `A judgement on which side is greater`, [`An equal list of both sides with no preference`, `A new advantage that you forgot earlier`, `A list of solutions to the disadvantages only`],
    `The word "outweigh" asks for a decision about which side is heavier. A conclusion that only lists both sides does not answer the question, and adding a brand-new point at the end weakens the structure.`],
  [2, 'question-type', `Which wording usually signals a discussion essay?`,
    `"Some people believe X, while others think Y. Discuss both views."`, [`"What are the causes of X, and how can it be solved?"`, `"Why is this happening? Is it a good thing?"`, `"X is a good idea. To what extent do you agree?"`],
    `Two opposing views plus the instruction to discuss them marks a discussion essay. The other wordings signal problem-solution, two-part questions and opinion essays in that order.`],
  [2, 'question-type', `A student answers "Some people think museums should be free. Discuss both views and give your opinion" by explaining only why museums should be free. What is the main weakness?`,
    `The other view is not discussed`, [`The essay is too long`, `The student gave an opinion`, `The essay has an introduction`],
    `The task asks for both views, so ignoring the argument for charging an entrance fee leaves part of the question unanswered. Giving an opinion is required, and an introduction is expected, so those are not weaknesses.`],
  [2, 'question-type', `The question is: "Why is this happening? What can be done about it?" A student writes three paragraphs about causes and one sentence about solutions. What is wrong?`,
    `Only half of the question is answered in enough detail`, [`Causes should never be mentioned`, `The essay should contain only solutions`, `Nothing is wrong, because causes are more important`],
    `Both parts of a question carry equal weight, so the essay must develop causes and solutions. A single sentence of solutions will not give enough reasoning or examples.`],
  [3, 'question-type', `Prompt: "Governments should spend more on public transport than on roads. To what extent do you agree?" Which approach follows the prompt best?`,
    `Compare the two kinds of spending and argue how far public transport should come first`, [`Write about public transport only, because it is better`, `Describe the history of roads`, `List problems of traffic and solutions to them`],
    `The words "than on roads" make this a comparison, so you must deal with both kinds of spending. Praising only public transport leaves the second half of the prompt out, and the other answers use a different task.`],
  [3, 'question-type', `Which prompt needs the longest plan for body paragraphs, with a separate paragraph for each part?`,
    `"Why do some people refuse to recycle? What could governments do to change this?"`, [`"Do you agree that homework should be banned?"`, `"Is it a good idea to ban advertising to children?"`, `"Do you agree that zoos should close?"`],
    `Two separate questions need a paragraph each: reasons first, then actions. The other prompts ask for one yes-or-no position with reasons.`],
  [3, 'question-type', `Which statement about "Discuss both views and give your own opinion" is correct?`,
    `Your own opinion is required, and it should be clear in the introduction and the conclusion`, [`Your opinion is optional if you describe both views well`, `You should leave your opinion out to sound objective`, `You should only give an opinion in the last sentence`],
    `Skipping the opinion loses marks for answering the whole task. A clear position early helps the reader follow the argument, and the conclusion should confirm it.`],
  [4, 'question-type', `Prompt: "Some people say that technology has made life more stressful. Others argue that it has made life easier. Discuss both views and give your opinion." Which plan matches the task?`,
    `Introduction with opinion; paragraph on stress; paragraph on ease; conclusion confirming the opinion`, [`Introduction; four paragraphs about stress only; conclusion`, `Introduction; paragraph on ease; paragraph about the history of computers; conclusion`, `Introduction without opinion; two paragraphs; conclusion with a new opinion`],
    `The right plan gives each view its own paragraph and states a consistent opinion. Plans that skip a view, wander into history or change the opinion at the end do not answer the prompt.`],

  // ---------- thesis (12) ----------
  [1, 'thesis', `Prompt: "Homework should be banned in primary schools. To what extent do you agree?" Which is the clearest thesis statement?`,
    `I strongly agree that homework should be banned in primary schools, because it adds stress without clear learning benefits.`, [`Homework is a topic that many people have different feelings about.`, `In this essay I will talk about homework.`, `Children in many countries receive homework every day.`],
    `The correct choice takes a position and gives a reason. The others only announce a topic, mention that opinions differ or state a fact, so the reader still does not know your answer.`],
  [2, 'thesis', `Prompt: "Some people prefer working from home, while others prefer an office. Discuss both views and give your opinion." Which thesis is clearest?`,
    `Although working from home offers flexibility, I believe that offices are better for new employees who need guidance.`, [`There are two views about working from home and both are interesting.`, `Working from home is good and bad.`, `Many people have worked from home since 2020.`],
    `It recognises the other view and still makes a clear choice with a reason. "Good and bad" refuses to choose, and the other two statements simply report or describe.`],
  [2, 'thesis', `Prompt: "Do the advantages of online shopping outweigh the disadvantages?" Which is the best thesis?`,
    `Overall, the advantages of online shopping outweigh its drawbacks, mainly because of convenience and choice.`, [`Online shopping has advantages and disadvantages.`, `Online shopping is very popular nowadays.`, `Some people like to shop online, but others do not.`],
    `The question asks which side is greater, so the thesis must say so and give a reason. The weaker options describe the topic and do not answer "do they outweigh".`],
  [3, 'thesis', `Prompt: "Child obesity is rising in many countries. What are the causes, and what can be done?" Which thesis previews a full answer?`,
    `Child obesity is caused mainly by inactivity and cheap junk food, and it can be reduced through school sport and clearer food labelling.`, [`Child obesity is a big problem today and we must solve it.`, `Many children are overweight in many countries.`, `This essay will explain child obesity.`],
    `It names causes and solutions in one sentence, so the reader knows the structure. "We must solve it" and "this essay will explain" promise nothing specific.`],
  [2, 'thesis', `Which statement does NOT state a clear position on space research?`,
    `There are many opinions about space research, and everyone has the right to their own view.`, [`Space research deserves public funding because it produces useful technology.`, `Governments should spend less on space research and more on hospitals.`, `Space research is worth the cost only when it has clear practical aims.`],
    `The first statement avoids saying what the writer thinks. The other three each commit to a view, even though they are different views.`],
  [3, 'thesis', `Prompt: "Some people think prison is the best way to deal with crime, others prefer rehabilitation. Discuss both views and give your opinion." Which thesis is best?`,
    `While prison protects the public from dangerous offenders, I believe rehabilitation is more effective for most crimes because it reduces reoffending.`, [`Prison and rehabilitation are both ways that governments deal with crime.`, `Prison is bad and rehabilitation is good.`, `Crime is a serious problem in modern society.`],
    `It acknowledges one view, picks the other and gives a reason. "Prison is bad" is a crude claim without a reason, and the others do not take a side.`],
  [1, 'thesis', `Where should your position appear in an opinion essay?`,
    `In the introduction, and again in the conclusion`, [`Only in the conclusion`, `Only in the middle of the essay`, `Nowhere, because the writer should stay neutral`],
    `Stating the position early gives the essay direction, and repeating it at the end confirms it. Keeping it hidden until the end makes the argument harder to follow.`],
  [3, 'thesis', `Prompt: "Universities should teach only subjects that lead directly to jobs. To what extent do you agree?" Which thesis shows a clear, balanced position?`,
    `I disagree to a large extent: job-related courses are valuable, but universities also need subjects that develop thinking and culture.`, [`I partly agree and partly disagree, and universities are important places.`, `Jobs are important for everybody in life.`, `Universities teach many subjects today.`],
    `It says how far the writer disagrees and why. "Partly agree and partly disagree" does not explain which part is which, and the last two are not answers.`],
  [2, 'thesis', `Prompt: "Why are more people choosing to be vegetarian? Is this a positive development?" Which thesis answers both questions?`,
    `More people are becoming vegetarian because of health and environmental concerns, and I believe this is a positive development.`, [`Vegetarian food is becoming more common in restaurants.`, `People choose different diets for different reasons.`, `I will discuss vegetarianism and whether it is positive.`],
    `It gives the reason and the judgement in one line. The other options describe the topic or promise to discuss it without answering either question.`],
  [3, 'thesis', `Which thesis is most suitable for a formal essay answering "Zoos should be closed. Do you agree?"`,
    `I disagree that zoos should be closed, since well-run zoos protect endangered species and educate the public.`, [`I reckon zoos are fine and everybody likes them.`, `Zoos are places where animals live in cages.`, `Zoos exist all over the world, which is a fact.`],
    `A formal thesis gives a clear position with reasons in neutral language. "I reckon" is informal and the other two give description, not an answer.`],
  [4, 'thesis', `Prompt: "Social media does more harm than good to young people. To what extent do you agree?" Which thesis is strongest?`,
    `I largely agree, because the constant comparison and lost sleep outweigh the benefits of staying in touch, although careful use can reduce the harm.`, [`Social media has many harmful and many good effects on young people.`, `Young people use social media every day for many hours.`, `Social media is new and it changes quickly.`],
    `The strongest thesis shows the degree of agreement, gives reasons and recognises a limit. Its rivals describe the situation without taking a stand.`],
  [4, 'thesis', `A student writes this thesis for an opinion essay: "I agree." What is the main weakness?`,
    `It gives no reason and no scope, so the reader cannot see how the argument will develop`, [`It is too long`, `It uses the word "I"`, `It contains an opinion`],
    `A one-word agreement shows a position but gives no direction. A good thesis adds a reason or a condition. First person is acceptable in an opinion essay, and giving an opinion is exactly the point.`],

  // ---------- introduction (12) ----------
  [2, 'introduction', `Which is the best paraphrase of: "Some people believe that the best way to reduce crime is to give longer prison sentences."`,
    `It is sometimes argued that lengthy jail terms are the most effective means of cutting crime.`, [`Some people believe that the best way to reduce crime is to give longer prison sentences.`, `Crime is a serious problem in many countries today.`, `Prison sentences should be shorter because they do not reduce crime.`],
    `The correct option keeps the meaning but uses new words and structure. The first copies the prompt, the second changes the subject, and the fourth reverses the meaning.`],
  [2, 'introduction', `Which is the best paraphrase of: "Children spend too much time looking at screens."`,
    `Young people devote an excessive amount of time to electronic devices.`, [`Children spend too much time looking at screens.`, `Children should not use phones or computers at all.`, `Young people enjoy watching television.`],
    `It changes the vocabulary but keeps the idea of too much time. The second is an extreme new claim, and the third changes the point to enjoyment.`],
  [1, 'introduction', `What should an introduction NOT contain?`,
    `A long, detailed example with names and figures`, [`A paraphrase of the question`, `Your position on the question`, `A short outline of your main points`],
    `Examples belong in body paragraphs, where they support a specific idea. An introduction should be brief: paraphrase, position and, if you like, a short outline.`],
  [1, 'introduction', `What is the main purpose of an introduction?`,
    `To show you understand the question and to state your answer`, [`To tell a story from your own life`, `To give all your examples at once`, `To repeat the question word for word`],
    `The introduction frames the topic in your own words and shows the reader your position. Stories and examples come later, and copying the question does not count as your own writing.`],
  [3, 'introduction', `Which introduction best answers: "Public libraries are no longer necessary because of the internet. To what extent do you agree?"`,
    `With so much information online, some people claim that public libraries have become unnecessary. I disagree, because libraries still serve people without internet access and provide shared community space.`, [`I am going to write about libraries. Libraries are places with books. I think they are good.`, `Public libraries are no longer necessary because of the internet, and I will say if I agree.`, `In today's modern society, technology is developing very fast and everybody uses the internet, so this essay will discuss it.`],
    `It paraphrases, takes a clear position and gives two reasons in two sentences. The weaker options either copy the prompt, wander into general statements or give no position.`],
  [2, 'introduction', `Which is the best way to paraphrase "young people"?`,
    `the younger generation`, [`children`, `adults`, `young peoples`],
    `"The younger generation" keeps the same group. "Children" is too narrow, "adults" is a different group, and "peoples" is not the right form here.`],
  [3, 'introduction', `A student paraphrases "Public transport should be free" as "Buses and trains should cost nothing." What is the problem?`,
    `It narrows the meaning, because public transport also includes trams and metros`, [`It is too long`, `It copies the original exactly`, `Paraphrases must always keep the same nouns`],
    `A paraphrase should keep the meaning. Replacing a general term with two specific examples changes its scope. Paraphrasing normally means changing words, not keeping them.`],
  [2, 'introduction', `Which is the best opening for: "Some people think museums should be free, while others think visitors should pay. Discuss both views and give your opinion."`,
    `Opinions differ on whether museums should charge entry or open their doors to all. This essay will discuss both views before explaining why I favour free entry.`, [`Museums are buildings with old objects inside them.`, `I like museums and I go every year with my family.`, `Museums should be free and that is my only opinion.`],
    `The first option paraphrases the debate, signals that both views will appear and shows the opinion. The others are irrelevant, personal or ignore one of the views.`],
  [4, 'introduction', `Why is "In today's modern world, technology is very important for everyone." a weak first sentence?`,
    `It is general enough to start any essay and does not address the specific question`, [`It is too short to be a sentence`, `It contains a grammar error`, `It is too formal for an essay`],
    `A sentence that could open any essay on technology does not show that you read this prompt. It is grammatically fine, and the formal tone is not the problem.`],
  [3, 'introduction', `Which sentence should be removed from this introduction? (1) Some people say that cities are too crowded. (2) Last summer I visited Mumbai and saw a crowded station with two thousand people. (3) I believe that city life still offers more benefits than problems. (4) This essay will explain why.`,
    `Sentence 2, the detailed personal example`, [`Sentence 1, the paraphrase`, `Sentence 3, the position`, `Sentence 4, the outline`],
    `A long personal anecdote with numbers belongs, if anywhere, in a body paragraph. Sentences 1, 3 and 4 are the paraphrase, the position and a signal of what comes next.`],
  [1, 'introduction', `How long is a good introduction in a 250-word essay?`,
    `Two or three sentences`, [`One full page`, `One word`, `Half of the essay`],
    `An introduction should be short, usually around 40 to 50 words, so there is space for developed body paragraphs. A very long one takes words from the argument.`],
  [4, 'introduction', `Which introduction for "Do the benefits of tourism outweigh the harm it causes?" avoids copying the prompt and still gives a position?`,
    `Visitors bring income to many regions, yet they can also damage the places they come to see. On balance, I believe the harm is greater unless numbers are managed.`, [`Do the benefits of tourism outweigh the harm it causes? I will answer this.`, `Tourism is a big industry and I like to travel to the beach.`, `The benefits of tourism outweigh the harm it causes, as the question says.`],
    `It restates both sides in new words and delivers a conditional judgement. The others copy the question or add personal statements.`],

  // ---------- topic-sentence (12) ----------
  [1, 'topic-sentence', `A paragraph explains that buses and trains carry many people at once, so fewer cars are on the road and less fuel is burned. Which topic sentence fits best?`,
    `Public transport reduces pollution because it moves many people with fewer vehicles.`, [`Pollution is bad.`, `Buses in London are red.`, `Many people drive to work every day.`],
    `A good topic sentence states the main idea of the paragraph clearly. "Pollution is bad" is too general, the bus colour is a detail, and driving to work does not match the paragraph.`],
  [2, 'topic-sentence', `A paragraph says language apps give instant feedback, can be used anywhere and cost less than courses. Which topic sentence fits?`,
    `Language apps make learning more flexible and affordable than traditional classes.`, [`Language apps are on many phones.`, `Learning languages is difficult for everyone.`, `Traditional classes were popular in the past.`],
    `It covers flexibility (anywhere, instant) and cost (cheaper) in one idea. The others are too general or point away from the content.`],
  [3, 'topic-sentence', `Which topic sentence is best for ONE paragraph?`,
    `Fines are an effective way to reduce littering in public places.`, [`Fines work, schools should teach children, and companies should use less packaging.`, `Littering.`, `I will now write about littering.`],
    `A single controlling idea is easier to develop. The first alternative contains three separate ideas, the second is not a sentence and the third only announces.`],
  [1, 'topic-sentence', `Which topic sentence is too broad to be useful?`,
    `Education is important.`, [`Free school meals improve concentration in class.`, `Evening classes help adults to change careers.`, `Small classes give teachers more time for each pupil.`],
    `"Education is important" could introduce anything, so the paragraph would have no focus. The others each limit the paragraph to one specific claim.`],
  [2, 'topic-sentence', `A paragraph describes noise, rising rents and litter caused by large numbers of visitors. Which topic sentence fits?`,
    `Tourism can damage the daily life of local residents.`, [`Tourism creates many jobs.`, `People travel for many reasons.`, `Beaches are beautiful places.`],
    `The details are all problems for residents, so the topic sentence must express that. Jobs would suit a different paragraph.`],
  [2, 'topic-sentence', `Which sentence is a topic sentence, not an example?`,
    `A major benefit of remote work is the time saved on commuting.`, [`For example, Sara saves two hours every day by working from her kitchen table.`, `Last year my uncle bought a laptop.`, `In one office I know, the desks stayed empty on Fridays.`],
    `The correct sentence makes a general claim that can be developed. The others are specific examples, which should follow the topic sentence.`],
  [1, 'topic-sentence', `Where does the topic sentence usually go in a body paragraph?`,
    `At the beginning`, [`At the very end of the essay`, `In the middle of an example`, `It is not needed`],
    `Starting with the main idea tells the reader what the paragraph is about. The explanation and example then support it.`],
  [2, 'topic-sentence', `A paragraph explains that older employees can train new staff, share knowledge and help others avoid mistakes. Which topic sentence fits?`,
    `Older employees can pass valuable experience on to younger colleagues.`, [`Older people are often retired.`, `Young employees use computers faster.`, `Many companies are located in cities.`],
    `Training, sharing and avoiding mistakes all show experience being passed on. The other sentences are unrelated or contradict the focus.`],
  [3, 'topic-sentence', `A paragraph suggests more night buses, better street lighting and taxi vouchers. Which topic sentence fits best?`,
    `Improving night-time transport would make cities safer for people returning home late.`, [`Cities have many streets.`, `Taxis are expensive and some are dirty.`, `People should stay at home at night.`],
    `All three measures concern safe travel at night. The other choices are off-topic, negative or not in line with the proposals.`],
  [4, 'topic-sentence', `For the question "Is it better to live in the countryside than in a city?", which topic sentence gives a controlled, arguable point for a body paragraph?`,
    `One clear advantage of rural life is the lower cost of housing.`, [`Rural life is different.`, `The countryside has fields and cows.`, `Cities have buildings and the countryside has trees.`],
    `It makes a specific claim that can be explained and exemplified. The others are vague descriptions with nothing to argue.`],
  [1, 'topic-sentence', `What should a body paragraph contain?`,
    `One main idea, explained and supported with an example`, [`Several unrelated ideas in a list`, `Only an example with no idea`, `A copy of the introduction`],
    `A focused paragraph states one idea, explains it and illustrates it. A list of different ideas is hard to follow, and an example alone shows nothing about its point.`],
  [4, 'topic-sentence', `Which topic sentence shows a link to the essay position "Governments should fund the arts"?`,
    `Public funding for the arts matters because private audiences alone cannot always support less popular forms of culture.`, [`Many artists have difficult lives.`, `Paintings can be very expensive.`, `Some people enjoy dancing.`],
    `The correct sentence supports the essay position with a reason. The others are true but do not move the argument forward.`],

  // ---------- support (12) ----------
  [2, 'support', `Point: "Online classes can save students money." Which sentence supports it best?`,
    `For instance, learners avoid paying for transport and accommodation, which can take up a large share of a student's budget.`, [`Online classes are a modern development.`, `Many students like pizza.`, `Money is important to everybody.`],
    `It explains how money is saved and names concrete costs. The other sentences are vague, off-topic or general.`],
  [1, 'support', `Which sentence is off-topic in a paragraph about the benefits of cycling? (1) Cycling improves fitness. (2) It costs less than a car because there is no fuel to buy. (3) Cities with cycle lanes often have fewer traffic jams. (4) Many people enjoy watching football at weekends.`,
    `Sentence 4`, [`Sentence 1`, `Sentence 2`, `Sentence 3`],
    `Sentence 4 is about football and has nothing to do with cycling. The other three each give a benefit of cycling.`],
  [2, 'support', `Which sentence is an example, not a general statement?`,
    `For example, a nurse who works night shifts can use flexible hours to spend more time with her children.`, [`Flexible hours help many workers.`, `Work-life balance is a modern concern.`, `Employers should understand their staff.`],
    `The correct sentence describes a specific person and situation. The others make general claims that still need support.`],
  [3, 'support', `Point: "Violent video games may affect the behaviour of young children." Which sentence best explains why?`,
    `Young children imitate what they see, so repeated violent scenes may make aggressive actions seem normal.`, [`Video games are sold in many shops.`, `Some games are very popular in Asia.`, `Parents buy games as gifts.`],
    `It gives a reason linking the cause (imitation) to the effect (normalised aggression). The others state facts that do not explain the claim.`],
  [3, 'support', `Which sentence is off-topic in a paragraph arguing that space research brings practical benefits? (1) Satellites help to forecast the weather. (2) Research into materials for spacecraft has led to better insulation. (3) Many people enjoy science-fiction films. (4) Satellite navigation depends on technology developed for space.`,
    `Sentence 3`, [`Sentence 1`, `Sentence 2`, `Sentence 4`],
    `Films may reflect public interest, but they are not a practical benefit of research. The other three each provide an everyday use.`],
  [2, 'support', `Which sentence is weak support because it is vague?`,
    `Many studies prove this, so it must be true.`, [`In a town without buses, elderly residents may struggle to reach the doctor.`, `If prices rise, families often buy less fresh food.`, `A student with a part-time job may have less time to revise.`],
    `"Many studies prove this" names no study and adds no information, so it adds nothing for the reader. The others describe realistic situations that illustrate a point.`],
  [3, 'support', `You cannot remember exact statistics for a point. What is the best way to support it?`,
    `Use a realistic example from general knowledge or experience, without precise figures`, [`Invent a precise percentage so it sounds scientific`, `Leave the point without support`, `Quote a famous person with words you are not sure they said`],
    `A believable example is allowed and helpful. Made-up figures or invented quotes are poor practice, and an unsupported point is weak.`],
  [2, 'support', `Point: "Public libraries help people who do not have internet at home." Which example is most relevant?`,
    `A job seeker without a computer can use the library's computers to send applications.`, [`Libraries often have quiet reading rooms.`, `Some libraries are very old buildings.`, `Libraries sell coffee in some towns.`],
    `The correct example shows exactly how a library helps someone with no internet. The others are facts about libraries that do not relate to access.`],
  [4, 'support', `In a paragraph arguing that tourism can raise prices for residents, which sentence does NOT help the argument?`,
    `Tourists often take excellent photographs of local landmarks.`, [`When landlords can earn more from holiday visitors, fewer flats are left for local tenants.`, `Shops may change their stock to suit visitors, so everyday goods become more expensive.`, `A restaurant near a famous square may raise its prices because customers are not local.`],
    `Photography is not related to prices. The other three give mechanisms by which tourism increases costs.`],
  [2, 'support', `After a topic sentence, what is the best way to develop the point?`,
    `Explain it and then give an example`, [`Repeat the topic sentence three times`, `Start a different topic`, `Give an example only, without saying what it shows`],
    `Explanation shows why the idea is true, and an example makes it concrete. Repetition adds no meaning, and a new topic loses focus.`],
  [3, 'support', `Choose the best sentence to follow: "Governments should fund art in schools because it develops creativity."`,
    `A child who paints or acts must invent new ways of expressing ideas, and this imagination later helps in fields such as science and business.`, [`Art is something that people have done for a long time.`, `Schools have many classrooms.`, `Some children prefer sport.`],
    `It explains how creativity develops and links it to a later benefit. The alternatives do not follow from the claim.`],
  [4, 'support', `Which supporting sentence gives a cause-and-effect explanation?`,
    `Because commuters can no longer find parking, many have started to use trains instead.`, [`Trains are usually painted in bright colours.`, `Parking is a word with two syllables.`, `Commuters come from many places.`],
    `"Because ... many have started to ..." links a cause to a result. The others are unrelated observations.`],
  [3, 'support', `A writer says "Fast food should be taxed." Which sentence adds the strongest explanation?`,
    `A tax would raise the price of unhealthy meals, which would encourage families to choose cheaper, healthier options and reduce pressure on hospitals.`, [`Fast food is sold in many restaurants.`, `Taxes exist in every country.`, `Some people like the taste of burgers.`],
    `It explains the mechanism and the possible result. The others give facts that do not prove or explain the point.`],

  // ---------- linking (12) ----------
  [1, 'linking', `Choose the best linker: "Public transport is cheap. ___ it is often slow."`,
    `However,`, [`Therefore,`, `For instance,`, `Moreover,`],
    `The second sentence contrasts with the first (cheap but slow), so "However," is correct. "Therefore" shows a result, which does not fit, and "Moreover" adds a similar point.`],
  [2, 'linking', `Choose the best linker: "He studied hard for months; ___ he passed the exam easily."`,
    `therefore,`, [`however,`, `whereas,`, `for instance,`],
    `The studying caused the result, so "therefore" fits. "However" would signal a contrast, and "whereas" compares two different things.`],
  [2, 'linking', `Choose the best word: "___ cars are convenient, they cause pollution."`,
    `Although`, [`Therefore`, `For instance`, `Moreover`],
    `"Although" joins two contrasting ideas in one sentence. The others are linking adverbs that do not work at the start of a clause in this way.`],
  [2, 'linking', `Choose the best word: "Cities offer many jobs, ___ villages offer peace and space."`,
    `whereas`, [`therefore`, `as a result`, `for instance`],
    `"Whereas" compares two different things. "Therefore" and "as a result" show consequences, and "for instance" would introduce an example.`],
  [1, 'linking', `Choose the best linker: "Exercise reduces stress. ___, it improves sleep."`,
    `Moreover`, [`However`, `Therefore`, `Although`],
    `The second sentence adds another benefit, which is the function of "Moreover". "However" would wrongly suggest a contrast.`],
  [1, 'linking', `Choose the best linker: "Many fruits are rich in vitamins. ___, oranges contain a lot of vitamin C."`,
    `For instance`, [`In contrast`, `Therefore`, `Although`],
    `Oranges are an example of vitamin-rich fruit, so "For instance" is right. "In contrast" would mean oranges are different.`],
  [2, 'linking', `Choose the best linker: "The factory closed last year. ___, hundreds of people lost their jobs."`,
    `As a result`, [`However`, `For example`, `Whereas`],
    `Losing jobs was the consequence of the closure. "However" would indicate contrast, and "whereas" belongs within a single sentence.`],
  [3, 'linking', `Which sentence is punctuated correctly?`,
    `Recycling is helpful; however, it is not enough.`, [`Recycling is helpful, however it is not enough.`, `Recycling is helpful however, it is not enough.`, `Recycling is helpful; however it, is not enough.`],
    `Two complete sentences joined by "however" need a semicolon or full stop before it and a comma after it. A comma alone creates a comma splice, and the others put the comma in the wrong place.`],
  [3, 'linking', `Choose the best word: "___ the high cost, many people still buy electric cars."`,
    `Despite`, [`Although`, `However`, `Because`],
    `"Despite" is followed by a noun phrase such as "the high cost". "Although" needs a full clause, "however" starts a new sentence and "because" shows the wrong relationship.`],
  [2, 'linking', `Choose the correct option: "She arrived late ___ the heavy traffic."`,
    `because of`, [`because`, `so that`, `although`],
    `"Because of" is followed by a noun phrase ("the heavy traffic"). "Because" would need a subject and a verb after it.`],
  [2, 'linking', `Which linking word is too informal for an academic essay?`,
    `Plus,`, [`Furthermore,`, `In addition,`, `Moreover,`],
    `"Plus" belongs to spoken English. The others are standard formal ways to add a point.`],
  [4, 'linking', `Which sentence uses the linker correctly?`,
    `Many cities have improved cycle lanes; consequently, more people now cycle to work.`, [`Many cities have improved cycle lanes; for instance, more people now cycle to work.`, `Many cities have improved cycle lanes whereas, more people now cycle to work.`, `Many cities have improved cycle lanes; although, more people now cycle to work.`],
    `"Consequently" shows a result and is punctuated correctly. "For instance" would introduce an example that is not provided, and "although" cannot follow a semicolon like this.`],

  // ---------- conclusion (12) ----------
  [2, 'conclusion', `Which conclusion best finishes an opinion essay that argued that online learning is worthwhile?`,
    `To sum up, although online learning has drawbacks, I believe that its flexibility makes it worthwhile for most students.`, [`To sum up, there are many opinions and nobody can be sure.`, `To sum up, a new point is that teachers should earn more.`, `To sum up, I have now finished writing.`],
    `It restates the position and the reason. The others avoid a position, add a new idea or only announce the end.`],
  [1, 'conclusion', `What should a conclusion NOT do?`,
    `Introduce a new main idea with a new example`, [`Restate your position`, `Summarise your main reasons`, `Be short`],
    `New ideas belong in body paragraphs where they can be developed. A conclusion should draw together what has already been said.`],
  [1, 'conclusion', `Which phrase is suitable for starting a conclusion?`,
    `To sum up,`, [`Firstly,`, `For instance,`, `Meanwhile,`],
    `"To sum up" signals the end. "Firstly" starts a list, "for instance" starts an example and "meanwhile" refers to something happening at the same time.`],
  [3, 'conclusion', `In the introduction, a writer said that they strongly agree that homework should be banned. Which conclusion is consistent?`,
    `In conclusion, because homework adds stress and little benefit, primary schools should ban it.`, [`In conclusion, homework is clearly essential, so schools should give more of it.`, `In conclusion, I have never been sure about homework.`, `In conclusion, teachers are underpaid and need more help.`],
    `The correct option repeats the position and the reason. The others contradict it, show doubt or switch to a different issue.`],
  [3, 'conclusion', `Which conclusion works best for a discussion essay on whether museums should be free?`,
    `In conclusion, although charging fees raises money, I believe that free entry is better because it opens culture to everyone.`, [`In conclusion, museums are buildings.`, `In conclusion, both sides are equal, so there is no answer.`, `In conclusion, some people pay and some do not.`],
    `It acknowledges the other view and then gives a clear opinion. Refusing to decide fails the instruction to give your own opinion.`],
  [1, 'conclusion', `How long should a conclusion usually be?`,
    `Two or three sentences`, [`One long paragraph as long as the body`, `A single word`, `Five full paragraphs`],
    `A short conclusion confirms the position without wasting words. If it is too long, it usually starts to repeat itself or add new ideas.`],
  [3, 'conclusion', `Which conclusion suits a problem-solution essay about plastic waste?`,
    `In summary, plastic waste harms wildlife and coastal communities, but stricter rules on packaging combined with better rubbish collection can reduce it significantly.`, [`In summary, plastic is made from oil.`, `In summary, I like using cloth bags.`, `In summary, there are no solutions.`],
    `It restates the main problem and the most effective solutions. The others give an unrelated fact, a personal preference or a contradiction of the task.`],
  [3, 'conclusion', `Which sentence is the weakest as the end of an essay?`,
    `As you can see, I have written about everything.`, [`Therefore, the benefits of public parks outweigh their costs.`, `For these reasons, I believe that tighter limits on advertising are justified.`, `Overall, stronger action is needed, though individuals also have a part to play.`],
    `It talks about the writing process and adds no meaning. The others finish with a clear point that answers the question.`],
  [2, 'conclusion', `You are running out of time. What is the best choice?`,
    `Write a short two-sentence conclusion that restates your position`, [`Skip the conclusion, because it is optional`, `Add one more body paragraph without a conclusion`, `Copy the introduction without change`],
    `A brief conclusion still completes the structure and confirms your view. Skipping it leaves the essay unfinished.`],
  [4, 'conclusion', `For a two-part question ("Why is this happening? Is it positive?"), which conclusion works best?`,
    `In summary, the trend is driven mainly by cost and convenience, and on balance I consider it positive.`, [`In summary, many trends exist in the world.`, `In summary, the reasons are interesting but I will not say if it is positive.`, `In summary, a third question is why nobody has studied this.`],
    `It answers both questions briefly. The others skip one question or add a new one.`],
  [4, 'conclusion', `Which conclusion repeats the introduction almost word for word, and so is the weakest?`,
    `In conclusion, some people think that a longer school day is useful, while others disagree, and I agree with the first group.`, [`In conclusion, longer school days may help those who need extra support, but the benefits depend on how the time is used.`, `To sum up, extending school hours seems justified only if lessons stay varied and teachers are not overworked.`, `Overall, more time at school can help, provided that quality does not suffer.`],
    `The first option almost copies the wording used to introduce the essay, so it adds nothing. The others rephrase the position with a condition.`],
  [2, 'conclusion', `Which sentence suits the end of a conclusion in an advantages-disadvantages essay?`,
    `Overall, the benefits outweigh the drawbacks, provided that the risks are managed.`, [`Meanwhile, here is another benefit I should have mentioned.`, `For instance, a friend of mine tried it.`, `However, the paragraph above is complete.`],
    `It gives the final judgement the question asks for. The others introduce new points or are not relevant to concluding.`],

  // ---------- register (12) ----------
  [1, 'register', `Which sentence is more suitable for a formal essay?`,
    `Children should be encouraged to read regularly.`, [`Kids gotta read loads, you know.`, `Kids should totally read heaps of stuff.`, `Reading is just brilliant, honestly.`],
    `The first is neutral and formal. The others use slang, vague quantities or casual comments that do not belong in academic writing.`],
  [2, 'register', `Which phrase best replaces "a lot of things" in a formal essay?`,
    `numerous factors`, [`loads of stuff`, `heaps of things`, `lots and lots`],
    `"Numerous factors" is precise and formal. The alternatives are informal and vague.`],
  [2, 'register', `You have used "I think" in every paragraph. Which opening varies the style while keeping the opinion?`,
    `It can be argued that cities need more green space.`, [`I think, I think cities need more green space.`, `Well, I just reckon so.`, `You know cities, they need green space.`],
    `"It can be argued that..." is a formal way to introduce an idea, so it avoids repetition. The others are repeated, casual or address the reader directly.`],
  [1, 'register', `Which sentence avoids contractions, as a formal essay should?`,
    `Governments cannot ignore this problem.`, [`Governments can't ignore this problem.`, `Governments won't ignore this problem.`, `Governments shouldn't ignore this problem.`],
    `Formal writing usually uses full forms, so "cannot" is preferred. The other options all contain contractions.`],
  [2, 'register', `Which sentence is more precise?`,
    `Many young adults struggle to afford rent in large cities.`, [`Lots of stuff is expensive for young people.`, `Things are bad for young people.`, `It is hard for some people in some places.`],
    `It names who, what and where. "Stuff" and "things" are vague, and the last option is too general to say anything.`],
  [3, 'register', `Which verb is the best formal alternative to "get" in "Graduates get good jobs"?`,
    `secure`, [`grab`, `snatch`, `fetch`],
    `"Secure" fits a formal context and the meaning. The others have different meanings or are informal.`],
  [3, 'register', `Which word best replaces "look into" in a formal sentence about a problem?`,
    `investigate`, [`peek at`, `check out`, `poke around`],
    `"Investigate" is a standard formal verb. The alternatives are informal phrasal verbs or idioms.`],
  [2, 'register', `Which sentence has an appropriate academic style?`,
    `Individuals should take responsibility for their own health.`, [`You have to look after yourself, right?`, `Isn't it obvious that everybody should stay healthy?`, `Look, people just need to get fit.`],
    `It is impersonal and direct. The others address the reader, ask a rhetorical question or use a casual tone.`],
  [3, 'register', `Which ending is better than "...jobs, careers, and so on"?`,
    `...jobs, careers and other aspects of working life.`, [`...jobs, careers, etc. etc.`, `...jobs, careers, and stuff like that.`, `...jobs, careers and whatever.`],
    `Naming the category is clearer and more formal than a vague ending. "Stuff like that" and "whatever" are informal.`],
  [3, 'register', `Which sentence is better because it avoids an over-generalisation?`,
    `Many people believe that exams cause too much stress.`, [`Everybody knows that exams cause too much stress.`, `All people hate exams.`, `Nobody learns anything from exams.`],
    `Absolute words like "everybody", "all" and "nobody" are rarely true and weaken the argument. "Many people believe" is safer and still makes the point.`],
  [4, 'register', `Which sentence uses hedging most appropriately for a claim that is probably but not always true?`,
    `Technology may contribute to loneliness in some cases.`, [`Technology always makes people lonely.`, `Technology obviously ruins everything.`, `Technology never helps anyone.`],
    `"May contribute" and "in some cases" are honest and measured. The other statements are absolute and easy to challenge.`],
  [2, 'register', `Which word is the best formal alternative to "big" in "a big problem"?`,
    `serious`, [`massive`, `gigantic`, `huge-sized`],
    `"Serious" or "significant" is the standard academic choice. "Massive" and "gigantic" are exaggerated for this context.`],

  // ---------- errors (12) ----------
  [1, 'errors', `Which sentence has no error?`,
    `An increase in tourism has changed the village.`, [`A increase in tourism has changed the village.`, `Increase in the tourism has changed the village.`, `The increase in tourism have changed the village.`],
    `"An" is needed before the vowel sound in "increase", and the verb must agree with the singular noun "increase". The other options have a wrong article or a wrong verb form.`],
  [2, 'errors', `Which sentence has no error?`,
    `The number of students has risen sharply.`, [`The number of students have risen sharply.`, `Each of the students are happy with the course.`, `Many students has joined the club.`],
    `"The number" is singular, so "has" is correct. "Each" takes a singular verb, and "many students" needs a plural verb, so the other sentences are wrong.`],
  [3, 'errors', `Which sentence has no error?`,
    `Many people drive to work; as a result, the roads are crowded.`, [`Many people drive to work, the roads are crowded.`, `Many people drive to work however the roads are crowded.`, `Many people drive to work. As a result the roads, are crowded.`],
    `A semicolon can join two complete sentences, followed by a linker with a comma. The first alternative is a comma splice, the second has no punctuation around "however", and the third misplaces a comma.`],
  [2, 'errors', `Which sentence has no error?`,
    `Pollution is increasing, so governments must act.`, [`Pollution is increasing governments must act.`, `Pollution is increasing, governments must act.`, `Pollution is increasing so, governments must act.`],
    `Two clauses need a joining word as well as a comma. Without it, the sentence is a run-on or a comma splice, and the comma after "so" is misplaced.`],
  [3, 'errors', `Which is a complete sentence?`,
    `Although exercise is important, many adults do very little.`, [`Although exercise is important.`, `Because many adults do very little exercise.`, `Which is a serious problem for health services.`],
    `The correct option has a main clause after the "Although" clause. The others are fragments because they begin with a subordinating word and lack a main clause.`],
  [2, 'errors', `Which sentence has no error?`,
    `Information about courses is available online.`, [`Informations about courses are available online.`, `Information about courses are available online.`, `The informations about courses is available online.`],
    `"Information" is uncountable, so it has no plural and takes a singular verb. The others use "informations" or a plural verb.`],
  [1, 'errors', `Which sentence has no error?`,
    `Last year the company opened three new branches.`, [`Last year the company opens three new branches.`, `Last year the company has opened three new branches.`, `Last year the company have opened three new branches.`],
    `A finished time such as "last year" calls for the simple past. Present simple and present perfect do not fit, and "company" with "have" is not standard here.`],
  [2, 'errors', `Which sentence has no error?`,
    `Students depend on their teachers for guidance.`, [`Students depend of their teachers for guidance.`, `Students depend from their teachers for guidance.`, `Students depend to their teachers for guidance.`],
    `"Depend on" is the fixed combination. The other prepositions are not used with this verb.`],
  [2, 'errors', `Which sentence has no error?`,
    `Fewer people now read printed newspapers.`, [`Less people now read printed newspapers.`, `Fewer peoples now read printed newspapers.`, `Fewer person now read printed newspapers.`],
    `"Fewer" is used with countable plural nouns such as "people". "Less" is used with uncountable nouns, and "peoples" or "person" are the wrong forms here.`],
  [3, 'errors', `Which sentence has no error?`,
    `Exercise improves both physical health and mental wellbeing.`, [`Exercise improves both physical health and mentally wellbeing.`, `Exercise improves both physically health and mental wellbeing.`, `Exercise improve both physical health and mental wellbeing.`],
    `Adjectives "physical" and "mental" modify the nouns correctly, and the verb agrees with "exercise". The others use an adverb in place of an adjective or the wrong verb form.`],
  [4, 'errors', `Which sentence has no error?`,
    `If governments invested more in rail, fewer people would drive.`, [`If governments will invest more in rail, fewer people would drive.`, `If governments invested more in rail, fewer people will drive.`, `If governments would invest more in rail, fewer people would drive.`],
    `The second conditional uses past simple in the if-clause and "would" in the result clause. The others mix tenses or use "would" in the if-clause.`],
  [3, 'errors', `Which sentence has no error?`,
    `The children's school is closed, and its teachers are on strike.`, [`The childrens' school is closed, and its teachers are on strike.`, `The children's school is closed, and it's teachers are on strike.`, `The childrens school is closed, and its teachers are on strike.`],
    `"Children" is already plural, so the possessive is "children's". "Its" shows possession, whereas "it's" means "it is".`],
];

export default {
  topic: 'writing-essay-skills',
  items: R.map(([level, sub, q, correct, wrongs, sol], i) => {
    const pos = (i * 3 + (i >> 2)) % 4, options = wrongs.slice(); options.splice(pos, 0, correct);
    return { id: 'wes-' + String(i + 1).padStart(3, '0'), type: 'mcq', level, exams: ['academic', 'general'], facets: { skill: 'writing', qtype: 'essaylang' }, sub, q, options, answer: pos, sol };
  }),
};
